# Real-Time Hand-Gesture Cursor Control System

A touchless human-computer interaction prototype that lets you control your computer's cursor using only hand gestures, tracked in real time through a standard webcam — no mouse or keyboard required.

This project is a practical, hands-on extension of independent research on vision-based gesture systems for motor-impaired users, and serves as a proof-of-concept for the "Cursor Mode" described in the author's final year project, *Hands-Free Computing: A Context-Aware Gesture and Voice Control System*.

## Features

| Gesture | Action |
|---|---|
| Pointing (index finger) | Move cursor |
| Fist (quick) | Left click |
| Fist (quick, twice) | Double click |
| Fist (held ~0.6s+) | Click-and-drag |
| Two fingers up (index + middle) | Scroll |

## Tech Stack

- **Python 3.12**
- **OpenCV** — video capture and frame processing
- **MediaPipe (Hands)** — real-time hand landmark detection
- **PyAutoGUI** — cursor and mouse action automation
- **NumPy** — coordinate mapping and smoothing

## How It Works

1. A webcam feed is captured and processed frame-by-frame using OpenCV.
2. MediaPipe detects 21 hand landmarks per frame in real time.
3. The palm center (landmark 9) is used as the stable reference point for cursor movement, so that finger gestures (like making a fist) don't disturb cursor position.
4. Webcam-space coordinates are mapped to full screen coordinates using an active-zone mapping technique, so a small, comfortable hand movement range covers the entire screen.
5. A smoothing algorithm reduces jitter for stable cursor movement.
6. Rule-based gesture classification (based on relative finger-joint positions) distinguishes between click, double-click, drag, and scroll gestures, with debouncing logic to avoid accidental gesture conflicts.

## Testing Results

Each gesture was tested over multiple trials in a normal indoor lighting environment:

| Gesture | Attempts | Successful | Accuracy |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cursor movement (pointing) | — | — | Smooth, real-time tracking with minimal jitter across the full screen range |
| Single Click (fist) | 20 | 18 | 90% |
| Double Click | 20 | 18 | 90% |
| Drag (held fist) | 15 | 15 | 100% |
| Scroll (two fingers) | 20 | 20 | 100% |

## Setup & Usage

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone <your-repo-url>
cd gesture-cursor-control

# Create and activate a virtual environment (Python 3.11 or 3.12 recommended)
py -3.12 -m venv gesture_env
gesture_env\Scripts\activate      # Windows
source gesture_env/bin/activate   # macOS/Linux

# Install dependencies
pip install opencv-python mediapipe==0.10.14 pyautogui numpy

# Run
python gesture_cursor.py
```

Press `q` to quit the application at any time.

**Note:** This project uses `mediapipe==0.10.14` specifically, as more recent MediaPipe releases have removed the legacy `solutions` API used here in favor of the newer Tasks API.

## Limitations & Future Work

- Currently supports a single hand and a fixed "Cursor Mode" only.
- Gesture recognition accuracy could be further improved with adaptive lighting handling.
- Planned extensions (per the associated final year project) include voice command integration and context-aware mode switching (Presentation / Media / Cursor).

## Background

This project extends independent research conducted by the author:
*A Review of Vision-Based Gesture Systems for Motor-Impaired Users (2020–2025)* — a systematic review of 24 studies on vision-based deep learning approaches for gesture-controlled assistive systems, conducted following PRISMA 2020 guidelines.

